78
00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:40.639
Derek Meyer: In terms of the general architecture, right, with RPC-based, indexing, right, there's the JSON RPC, which the block gets produced, you then go and, after the production of the block, you then send the request, you get your JSON object back.

79
00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:56.170
Derek Meyer: That gets stored in your database, then you need to transform that information, and then ultimately load that into whatever your application is. Going from, you know, to use metals, we'll say you have your bronze layer, your silver layer, and then your gold layer.

80
00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:01.339
Derek Meyer: with, with Firehose, and specifically with substreams.

81
00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:17.620
Derek Meyer: What happens is, there's a little bit of code in the blockchain node that will then output all of the, all of the block, whether it's the event logs, the internal transactions, state changes, all the different components inside of the block. It outputs it

